      "claim": "Items contaminated with unknown or hazardous substances during an investigation cannot be safely reused without specific decontamination protocols.",
      "evidence": "Standard laboratory safety protocols dictate that materials exposed to unknown chemicals or biological agents are considered contaminated and pose a risk of cross-contamination or exposure.",
      "weight": 0.9,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "claim": "Filter paper (Option B) and paper towels (Option C) are porous materials that absorb liquids and chemicals, making them difficult or impossible to decontaminate effectively for reuse.",
      "evidence": "Porous materials retain contaminants in their fibers, posing a persistent hazard if reused in a new investigation.",
      "weight": 0.8,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "Powdered chemicals (Option D) are consumable reagents that are used up or contaminated during the investigation and cannot be 'reused' in the same context; they are typically disposed of as waste.",
      "evidence": "Chemical reagents are not typically recovered and reused in the same experiment due to purity loss and contamination risks.",
      "weight": 0.7,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "Glassware (Option A) is non-porous and can be thoroughly cleaned and sterilized to remove contaminants, allowing it to be safely reused in subsequent investigations.",
      "evidence": "Standard laboratory practice involves washing glassware with detergent and water, followed by rinsing, to ensure it is free of residues before reuse.",
      "weight": 0.9,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "Therefore, glassware is the only item listed that can be safely reused after an investigation.",
      "evidence": "Comparison of reusability: Glassware (reusable after cleaning) vs. Filter paper/Paper towels (single-use/disposable) vs. Chemicals (consumable/waste).",
      "weight": 1.0,
      "depends_on": [2, 3, 4]
    }
  ],
  "answer": "A",
  "confidence": 0.95,
  "considered": [
    {
      "answer": "B",
      "reason_rejected": "Filter paper is porous and retains contaminants; it is generally considered single-use in scientific investigations to prevent cross-contamination."
    },
